  "articleBody": "It's easy to assume that a back entrance isn't subject to the same accessibility rules as your main entrance, especially if you think only employees use it. But here's the thing: sometimes, even if the public doesn't generally use a back door, if employees regularly do, or if it provides access to an area undergoing an alteration, it might need to meet certain accessibility standards. It's not always a straightforward answer, and simply calling it a 'back door' doesn't automatically grant an exemption. You'll want to assess who's actually using that entrance and for what purpose. Sometimes, the path of travel to an altered area might trigger requirements for making another entrance accessible. We've seen situations where a small renovation in one part of a building unexpectedly leads to needing accessibility improvements at an entrance you hadn't considered. Don't overlook employee use, as that's a common oversight. If you're altering a primary function area, you might also have path-of-travel obligations that include making another entrance accessible. It's really about taking a holistic look at how your space is used and what other work is being done. When you're dealing with these kinds of triggered requirements, or if the classification of your route or entry is unclear, that's definitely a good time to reach out to a professional who can help you navigate the specific codes and ensure everything is up to par.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
